[ ================================================================ ]
[ 060 -- functions                                                 ]
[                                                                  ]
[     phoenix examples/060_functions.phx                           ]
[ ================================================================ ]

[ `def name { body }` defines a function, and `$name` calls it.     ]
[ There are no parameter lists and no return statement: a function  ]
[ takes its arguments off the stack and leaves its results there.   ]

def newline { 10 printa }

def square {
	var n  n !      [ take one argument off the stack ]
	n n *           [ leave one result on it ]
}

7 $square print $newline

[ Expected output:                                                  ]
[                                                                   ]
[     49                                                            ]

[ With more than one argument the LAST one pushed is taken off      ]
[ first, so unpacking them in reverse lines them up with the order  ]
[ they were written at the call.                                    ]

def difference {
	var second  second !     [ second was on top, so it comes off first ]
	var first   first !
	second first -           [ by the rule from 020, this is first - second ]
}

10 3 $difference print $newline

[ Expected output:                                                  ]
[                                                                   ]
[     7                                                             ]

[ !! A function body is not run where it is written -- the compiler ]
[ jumps over it and the call goes back to it later. So a function   ]
[ may be called on a line above the one that defines it.            ]

$greet

def greet {
	33 105 72  3 $puts      [ see `puts` below for the reversal ]
	$newline
}

[ !! `def` cannot be nested. Functions are global, so an inner one  ]
[ would silently become visible everywhere; the compiler rejects it ]
[ rather than pretend otherwise.                                    ]

[ Here is the helper promised back in 000. It prints a run of       ]
[ characters, so push them in REVERSE and then how many there are.  ]
[ The reversal is because the stack hands them back top first.      ]

def puts {
	var n  n !
	dow {
		printa
		1 n - n !
		0 n =
	}
}

[ P h o e n i x ! is 80 104 111 101 110 105 120 33, backwards.      ]
33 120 105 110 101 111 104 80  8 $puts
$newline

[ Expected output:                                                  ]
[                                                                   ]
[     Hi!                                                           ]
[     Phoenix!                                                      ]
[                                                                   ]
[ `Hi!` comes first because `$greet` is called before this line,    ]
[ even though `def greet` is written below it.                      ]

[ Writing a string still means looking up the codes, but at least   ]
[ it is one line now.                                               ]
